  "live_records": {
    "q-39499": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39499",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250658/assets/ad5f193a1ff21e03659789473a6e4918b6dd7ba25cd9db4e6fc0f729d0481391.png)\n\nWhat are the equations of the horizontal asymptotes of $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "- On the right of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets larger and larger, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $-3.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to -3\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a horizontal asymptote at $y=-3.$\n\n- On the left of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets more and more negative, the function $f(x)$ levels off to the value of $2.$ We can express this as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\to 2\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to -\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ also has a horizontal asymptote at $y=2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
